      "message": "Good catch. However, blacklist-nvme.conf remains after the test and survives reboots. If the test is killed before teardown, the file is never removed.\n\nConsider pausing udev event processing while swapping drivers. This breaks the race in the critical section (driver swaps) regardless of events that triggers the nvme load (the bind events from dnvme, coldplug add event that might be queued if the test starts right after boot). And it leaves no persistent state.\n\nsubprocess.check_call([\"udevadm\", \"control\", \"--stop-exec-queue\"])\ntry:\n    os.system(\"rmmod nvme\")\n    subprocess.check_call([\"insmod\", \"dnvme.ko\"])\nfinally:\n    subprocess.check_call([\"udevadm\", \"control\", \"--start-exec-queue\"])\nsubprocess.check_call([\"udevadm\", \"settle\"])\n\nnvme.ko may still be loaded once the queue resumes but it no harm is caused - dnvme already owns all controllers. However, it means the /sys/bus/pci/drivers/nvme assertion has to be replaced with a check that every NVMe controller (PCI class 0x010802) is bound to dnvme.",
